stage: 4
finding: P1 — Statistical finding remains open: fallback does not inherit the family quantile. bin/step.ts tolerance(b,k) returns b.range, ignoring k, when normality is rejected; 299 is the 0.99/0.95 count for ONE quantity with ALL 299 observations. Consequently the majority nonnormal quantities have no schedule-wide 95% probability guarantee. If m fallback quantities each had exactly 0.01 tail probability and independent fresh outcomes, probability of at least one exceedance would be 1 - 0.99^m (near 1 for thousands), expected exceedances 0.01m. Independence is not established; without it the union bound is min(1,0.01m), not 0.05. This is an illustration of what is allowed, not a measured false-red rate: power-of-two rounding and the floor may lower it. Likewise no false-pass/port-bug rate follows at all without a distribution/model of bugs. Passing fault probes does not establish one. The parameter-confidence budget is also still per quantity: deviationInflation uses chi2_0.05 independently rather than a family confidence allocation. Accepting Anderson-Darling does not prove a normal tail at ~0.05/N, and the estimated absolute mean is not an upper confidence bound on the true mean. Further, kept runs may number below 299; falling back on fewer than eight kept observations gives no 0.99/0.95 range guarantee. These are not repaired by increasing only the CNH normal quantile. To claim a distribution-free family guarantee, predeclare probability/confidence budgets for all events (including decisions and conditioned samples), use sufficient actual observations for them, or calibrate a predeclared joint maximum statistic over independent complete replicates. For scalar Bonferroni allocations p_j=1-0.05/N, alpha_j=0.05/N, an all-success range would need ceil(log(alpha_j)/log(p_j)) observations per quantity, not 299. Alternatively explicitly abandon the simultaneous guarantee and establish an accepted, quantified heuristic contract with the architect/person. P2 — Qualification command is not a reliable exit-status gate. shallot-gpu-physics package.json runs QUALIFY_ORACLE=1 bun test --todo; the full humanoid remains test.todo (tests/step-oracle.gpu.test.ts:441-443). The author's recorded successful hold exits 1; an expected todo failure is not a normal failing-test gate. A humanoid regression can therefore remove the unexpected-pass signal instead of producing a conventional red. The full qualification must run this callback as a real test (it may remain deferred outside qualification), or explicitly validate structured outcomes, not treat exit 1 as successful qualification. Short/full budget separation is otherwise reasonable; no request to repeat a 51-minute full run was necessary for this finding. Fixed: prior missing-sqrt coverage finding is resolved by the link-time wrapper, -fno-builtin-sqrtf and inline-root disassembly guard. Independently ran sqrt-neighbour distribution/exact-root/upstream-routing test, 897 IEEE/native step comparisons, seeded repeatability, constants/AD and replacement independent CNH sample test: 6 tests pass, 944 assertions, 2.42s. The old independently failing mean regression is replaced and no longer reproduced; its replacement provides useful empirical evidence, not proof of the family claim. This run reported 171/271 tested quantities nonnormal. Examined c5a97e6 and f0d3343 with their specified predecessor commits and supplied reports. Inspected pinned-image Box3D timer call sites: stage timing writes profile fields, not the physics inputs/output; freezing the linked clock removes irrelevant PRNG-stream consumption and has no identified physics change. Repeatability test passes. Remains a fixed-stream experiment, not evidence for a schedule's stochastic coverage. No edits or pre-land check; full qualification and hosted CI not independently rerun. Source basis remains Sohier et al. arXiv:1807.09655 sections 4/5 and Verificarlo's implementation/docs.
